<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Android 12+ (API 31+) splash-screen API.

      On API 31+ the legacy windowBackground splash is ignored and the OS
      generates the boot splash itself. Without these attributes it falls back
      to the launcher icon and crops it against the circular splash mask.

      Three layers, and the middle one is new:

        windowSplashScreenBackground          @color/brand_plate  (navy)
        windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor @color/ic_launcher_background
                                              (white — the ground the art
                                              is drawn on)
        windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon        @drawable/android12splash

      The brand art changed from white-and-orange on a navy plate to yellow and
      blue on WHITE. Its wordmark measures rgb(0,66,143), which on the old navy
      splash plate is a 1.7:1 contrast ratio — it would have simply vanished.
      So the icon now floats on a white disc, which is the ground it was drawn
      against, while the splash field around it stays the navy brand plate so
      the handoff into splash_screen.dart's navy gradient is still seamless.
      Making the whole splash white instead would have reinstated exactly the
      white-flash-then-dark-screen this file was written to remove.

      The white disc is 240dp inside the OS's 288dp canvas and the icon ink
      sits inside 0.611 x 288 = 176dp, so the disc comfortably contains it.
    -->
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/launch_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/brand_plate</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor">@color/ic_launcher_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/android12splash</item>
    </style>
    <style name="NormalTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">?android:colorBackground</item>
    </style>
</resources>
